// Specifications about a linked artifact no Inference compiler produced. All
// three externals arrive from `tests/test_data/wasmlib/rustlib.wasm`, the
// committed output of `cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`,
// and are spliced in by the static-merge linker under the name section symbols
// `rustlib::clamp_add`, `rustlib::mulhi` and `rustlib::sum_n`, which is what the
// obligations apply.
//
// The sibling `spec_linked_extern.inf` already puts a merged body in front of
// `coqc`, but its external came out of this compiler's own emitter, so every
// instruction in it is one some single-file fixture emits too. What this fixture
// adds is bodies written by a different compiler: LLVM's branchless clamp, a
// pointer-walking loop whose `BI_loop` carries a result type, and the width
// conversions of a 32x32 high-product. None of the three has an emitter arm here
// — this compiler never selects, its `while` lowering always writes
// `BT_valtype None`, and it emits no conversion at all — so translating them is
// the claim the linker envelope rests on.
//
// `mulhi` is why `BI_cvtop` reaches `coqc` from a real artifact rather than only
// from a hand-assembled module. Its 64-bit intermediate is load-bearing, which is
// what keeps `i64.extend_i32_s` and `i32.wrap_i64` alive through optimization;
// `clamp_add` is also written over `i64` and is narrowed back to `i32` entirely.
//
// Two constraints this fixture is written around, both of which would otherwise
// surface as a confusing failure elsewhere:
//
//   - It must declare no linear memory of its own. The external declares
//     sixteen pages, a main declares its memory with a fixed maximum, and the
//     merge keeps the larger minimum inside the kept maximum — so a main with
//     the default single page cannot take this external at all. Staying
//     memoryless means every local here is a scalar: no array, struct or string.
//   - The corpus-wide checks reject a data segment in any module they compile,
//     which is the same restriction seen from the other side.
//
// Both obligations were chosen to be true of the merged bodies — adding zero
// saturates nowhere, and a zero-length walk reads nothing — but nothing here
// establishes that. The `coqc` gate compiles the generated direct `Admitted.`
// stubs, so it checks that the obligations are well-typed and says nothing
// about whether they hold; a false one would pass it just as quietly. Closing
// that needs a discharge against the real verifier, as the reachability and
// aggregate fixtures got.

external fn clamp_add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32;
external fn mulhi(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32;
external fn sum_n(p: i32, n: i32) -> i32;
use { clamp_add, mulhi, sum_n } from rustlib;

pub fn saturating_add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
  return clamp_add(a, b);
}

pub fn high_product(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
  return mulhi(a, b);
}

pub fn total(p: i32, n: i32) -> i32 {
  return sum_n(p, n);
}

spec ToolchainSpec {
  fn adding_zero_changes_nothing() forall {
    let a: i32 = @;
    assert(clamp_add(a, 0) == a);
  }

  fn multiplying_by_zero_carries_nothing_high() forall {
    let a: i32 = @;
    assert(mulhi(a, 0) == 0);
  }

  fn summing_nothing_is_zero() forall {
    let p: i32 = @;
    assert(sum_n(p, 0) == 0);
  }
}
